eclared: .changedFiles, returned: (.files | length)}'\n# => {\"declared\":115,\"returned\":100}\n```\n\nThe defence is that the true count usually sits in a sibling scalar field — so ask for both and\ncompare. `changedFiles` pairs with `files`; `subIssues`, `blockedBy`, and `blocking` are shaped\n`{\"nodes\": [...], \"totalCount\": N}` (capped at 100, 50, and 50) and carry their own `totalCount`.\nIf the numbers disagree, page through `gh api --paginate` instead of trusting the array. Saying\n\"this PR touches 100 files\" when it touches 115 is the kind of error nobody catches downstream.\n\n### Target the repo explicitly\n\n`gh` infers the repo from the cwd's git remotes. Pass `--repo OWNER/REPO` (`-R`) whenever you're\nacting on a repo other than the one you're standing in — or whenever you're not certain which that\nis. Do the same for `gh api`: the `{owner}/{repo}` placeholder is auto-filled from detected remotes,\nwhich is convenient interactively and a liability in a script. Write the values literally when the\nanswer needs to be deterministic.\n\n### Don't fight the terminal\n\n`gh` already behaves correctly in non-TTY contexts — it skips the pager, strips color, and fails\nfast with a useful message instead of prompting. There is no `--no-pager` flag and setting\n`GH_PAGER` defensively is unnecessary. If you *want* TTY-style tables and color, set `GH_FORCE_TTY=1`\ndeliberately.\n\n## Reading files from a repo without cloning\n\nTwo calls answer most \"what's in that repo\" questions, and neither needs a working copy. Reach for\nthem before cloning anything — a clone pays for an entire checkout, and the question usually turns\nout to need two or three named files.\n\nStart with structure, so you're choosing paths instead of guessing them. One call returns every path\nwith its blob size:\n\n```bash\ngh api 'repos/OWNER/REPO/git/trees/HEAD?recursive=1' \\\n  --jq '.tree[] | select(.type==\"blob\") | \"\\(.size)\\t\\(.path)\"'\n```\n\nCheck the sibling `truncate