fore* the code is written,\nand there is no non-interactive way to split a finished branch afterwards (`gh stack modify` is\nTUI-only). By the time you are reaching for `gh pr create`, the cheap moment has passed.\n\nInvoke it when a stack is **mentioned** — stack, layers, dependent or chained PRs, `gh stack`, or a\nrequest to split work for review — and also when one is **justified** by the shape of the work, even\nunprompted: two or more concerns where a later one depends on an earlier one (schema → API → UI,\nimplementation → its tests), parts with different reviewer audiences, or a part already large enough\nto review on its own. The skill's own test is that a layer you cannot describe in one sentence is\nusually two layers. None of this applies to single-concern work, one-file fixes, or anything that\nreviews fine as one PR, and unrelated changes want a separate stack rather than another layer in this\none. Err toward invoking it: reading the skill costs a few hundred lines, while discovering at review\ntime that five concerns are welded into one branch costs a manual teardown and rebuild. Invoke it\nrather than assuming it is loaded — load order is not guaranteed and it does not fire on `gh pr`\nphrasing.\n\nThree commands below are actively wrong on a stacked branch, not merely suboptimal:\n`gh pr create --base main` sets the wrong base, so the PR's diff swallows every layer beneath it;\n**`gh pr merge` cannot merge a stack at all**, and `--delete-branch` breaks the chain; and\n`gh pr checkout` fetches one branch without its stack, losing local tracking. Use `gh stack submit`,\n`gh stack merge <target> --yes`, and `gh stack checkout` instead. The rest of this section is safe on\na stacked PR — `view`, `checks`, `diff`, and `edit` behave normally, and gh-stack itself points at\n`gh pr edit` for retitling the PRs that `submit --auto` generates. When unsure whether a branch is\nstacked, `gh stack view --json` exits **2** with \"not part of a stack\" and costs nothing.\n\nCreate a PR with a multi-line body (the `--body` flag mangles `\\n` into literal characters — always use `--body-file`):\n\n```bash\ncat > /tmp/pr-body.md << 'EOF'\n## Summary\nDescription here.\nEOF\ngh pr create --base main --head <branch> --title \"<title>\" --body-file /tmp/pr-body.md\n```\n\nIf you must use inline text, use shell ANSI-C quoting: `--body $'line1\\n\\nline2'`\n\nCheck CI status:\n```bash\ngh pr checks <number>\n```\n\nView a PR with specific fields:\n```bash\ngh pr view <number> --json title,state,reviews,statusCheckRollup\n```\n\nCheckout a PR locally:\n```bash\ngh pr checkout 123\n```\n\nView PR diff:\n```bash\ngh pr diff 123\ngh pr diff 123 --name-only  # file list only\n```\n\nMerge a PR:\n```bash\ngh pr merge 123 --squash --delete-branch   # squash merge + cleanup\ngh pr merge 123 --rebase --delete-branch   # rebase merge + cleanup\n```\n\nNeither form works on a stacked PR — `gh stack merge <target> --yes` is the only thing that can,\nand it merges every unmerged PR below the target too, all-or-nothing.\n\nEdit PR metadata:\n```bash\ngh pr edit 123 --title \"New title\" --add-label bug --add-reviewer user1\n```\n\n## CI / GitHub Actions\n\nList recent workflow runs:\n```bash\ngh run list --limit 10\n```\n\nView a failed run and its logs:\n```bash\ngh run view <run-id>\ngh run view <run-id> --log-failed\n```\n\nWatch a run until it completes (use `--exit-status` to get non-zero exit on failure):\n```bash\ngh run watch <run-id> --exit-status\n```\n\n## PR Reviews\n\n### Fetching review threads\n\nDelegate bulk fetching to the **`pr-review-fetcher` agent**. It already owns the paginated GraphQL\nquery and the filtering vocabulary (unresolved, outdated, by author, by date, by file), and it runs\non a cheap model in its own context — so a hundred review threads never land in yours. It is\nread-only by design; come back here for the mutations (reply, resolve, comment) below.\n\n**If you *are* `pr-review-fetcher`**, this paragraph is des