ed 3.19+. On older servers, or if\na flag is missing, drive the API directly. The trap there is that these endpoints take the\n**internal integer ID**, not the issue number — and `gh issue view --json id` returns the GraphQL\nnode ID, which is a *different* wrong value. Fetch the integer explicitly:\n\n```bash\nCHILD_ID=$(gh api repos/OWNER/REPO/issues/CHILD_NUMBER --jq '.id')\ngh api repos/OWNER/REPO/issues/PARENT_NUMBER/sub_issues -X POST -F \"sub_issue_id=$CHILD_ID\"\ngh api repos/OWNER/REPO/issues/PARENT_NUMBER/sub_issues --jq '.[] | {number, title, state}'\ngh api repos/OWNER/REPO/issues/CHILD_NUMBER/parent --jq '{number, title}'\n\nBLOCKER_ID=$(gh api repos/OWNER/REPO/issues/42 --jq '.id')\ngh api repos/OWNER/REPO/issues/43/dependencies/blocked_by -X POST -F \"issue_id=$BLOCKER_ID\"\ngh api repos/OWNER/REPO/issues/43/dependencies/blocked_by/$BLOCKER_ID -X DELETE\n```\n\nThere is no \"set parent\" endpoint — you add a child to a parent, which is the same relationship\napproached from the other side.\n\n</details>\n\n## Search\n\n`gh search` uses GitHub's search index and takes each qualifier as **its own bare token**. Passing\nthe query as one quoted string is the single most damaging mistake here, because it does not error\n— the whole string is parsed as one keyword and you get an empty result set with exit code 0, which\nreads exactly like \"there are none\":\n\n```bash\ngh search issues repo:cli/cli is:open author:monalisa   # correct\ngh search issues \"label:bug state:open\"                 # WRONG — silently returns nothing\ngh search issues repo:cli/cli \"pagination\"              # quote only multi-word free text\n```\n\nOne exception to \"bare tokens\": quote any qualifier containing a shell metacharacter, or the shell\neats it before `gh` ever sees it. `stars:>1000` unquoted is a **redirect** — it writes a file named\n`1000` and passes an empty `stars:`, which `gh` then rejects with a confusing message about numeric\nvalues. Quote that token alone, n